+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes are delivered to connected browsers over **self-hosted WebSockets**: the server publishes each committed revision to the sockets attached to that event's board. This yields fast updates without a recurring realtime-vendor fee or provider coupling, at the cost of owning connection capacity, reliable publication, and reconnect behavior. Subsecond updates are the expected steady state; the 15-second freshness goal remains the floor under degraded connectivity.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Three consequences follow for the nodes below:
+- Change store: revisions must be replayable — a reconnecting browser can catch up from a stored revision, so the store holds enough history (within the seven-day retention) to serve catch-up reads and ordered publication.
+- Delivery: owns WebSocket connection capacity and session lifecycle (attach, detach, authentication of the socket), and publishes committed revisions reliably to attached sessions.
+- Browser sync: owns reconnect and catch-up logic — on a dropped or missed stream, the browser recovers the latest board rather than assuming silence means "unchanged" (per the visibility tenet).
 
